    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039522": {
      "title": "PhD Researcher in Sociology",
      "description": "Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Trinity College Dublin- an introduction Post Summary The candidate will join the PhD programme at the Department of Sociology of Trinity College Dublin, Ireland’s leading university which is located on a historic campus in the heart of Dublin. The Department will provide all logistic, academic and intellectual support and integrate the successful candidate into a vibrant doctoral programme at TCD. PhD students at the department are embedded in the interdisciplinary context of the School of Social Sciences and Philosophy which also includes economics, political science, and philosophy. PhD students in Sociology enjoy a wide range of training opportunities. For example, students participate in a research training programme in the department and have the opportunity to join masters level courses (including courses on research methods). Moreover, our PhD students are supported to attend high-quality international workshops and summer schools. PhD students are expected to collaborate closely with their supervisors and will produce a PhD based on four papers of a publishable standard. PhD students may also have the opportunity to gain teaching experience by providing support within our undergraduate programme (up to 4 hours per week during term time, subject to availability). All teaching-related work is paid separately in addition to the stipend. Application Procedure Informal enquiries are welcome and can be made directly to Associate Professor Camilla Devitt, Department of Sociology at Trinity College Dublin; devittca@tcd.ie To apply, candidates should submit the following via email to Camilla Devitt, Associate Professor (devittca@tcd.ie) in a single pdf : 1. A motivation letter clearly indicating motivation and background (max. 1 page) 2. A full curriculum vitae 3. A concise research statement no longer than 1500 words excluding references (see more below) 4. The names and contact details of two referees (with email addresses included); 5. At least one example of your written work (in English and including some data analysis) that shows your analytical and writing skills in a social science discipline. Applications must indicate “PhD Application in Sociology” in the subject line. Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. We welcome applications from all individuals, including those with disabilities, those who have followed non-traditional career paths, and those who have taken time out for reasons such as family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants, including those who have been displaced due to conflict or war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier university. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and hold an Athena Swan Silver Award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and across the higher education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work–life balance and creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039507": {
      "title": "PhD Studentship",
      "description": "Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Developing a new generation of dynamic socio-marine ecosystem models Marine and coastal socio-ecosystems such as Dublin Bay are highly complex systems subject to accelerated climate change. They are characterised by interacting biological, physical and human processes operating across very different spatial and temporal scales. Climate change will alter not only environmental conditions but also habitat configurations, population dynamics, species interactions, food-web structures and human behaviour. Compoundingly, coastal ecosystems are shaped by strong interactions between benthic and pelagic processes, collectively referred to as bentho–pelagic coupling. These interactions regulate nutrient cycling, carbon transfer, trophic connectivity, and ecosystem productivity, whilst remaining highly 2 sensitive to climate change and anthropogenic pressures. Dublin Bay provides an ideal natural laboratory due to its history of eutrophication, wastewater inputs, urbanisation, and ongoing environmental recovery and management interventions. This PhD will investigate how these processes might be represented in an innovative mathematical manner, without assuming that the ecosystem structure itself remains fixed. Thus, it will delve into the question as to how we might conceptualise and mathematically structure marine socio-ecosystem models if the structure of the system itself can change through time and space. Rather than treating climate change simply as a process that “nudges” an existing ecosystem, we want to investigate modelling frameworks capable of representing emergent, adaptive and potentially reorganising marine socio-ecosystems. The project will explore new mathematical and computational approaches to represent such systems, drawing on ideas from stochastic processes, four-dimensional (space-time) modelling, complex systems, agent-based modelling and artificial intelligence. An important component of the PhD will be determining which combinations of approaches are scientifically useful, rather than applying AI or other computational techniques for their own sake. The project will be supervised by Prof. Karen Wiltshire, and co-supervised by Dr. Erica Terese Krueger (Trinity College Dublin), (Dr.) Michael Mitchell (Trinity College Dublin) and Dr. Sabine Horn (Alfred Wegener Institute). Application Procedure • Applicants should submit a full Curriculum Vitae to include the names and contact details of 2 referees (including email addresses), to: Prof. Karen Wiltshire Karen.Wiltshire@tcd.ie Our Values - putting our words into actionValues matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. Trinity welcomes applications from all individuals, including those applicants with disabilities, those who may have had non-traditional career paths, those who have taken time out for reasons including family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants including those who have been displaced due to war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier University. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and we hold an Athena Swan Silver award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and in the Higher Education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work-life balance and to creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039551": {
      "title": "PhD Studentship",
      "description": "Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Post Summary : PhD applications are invited to develop advanced neuroimaging biomarkers of Alzheimer’s disease in mid-life, asymptomatic individuals. The PhD student’s role will include designing and performing all relevant analyses to assess (a) the performance of established and novel pre-clinical neuroimaging markers of AD in mid-life in persons at high- relative to low-risk, and (b) the interaction between changes in neuroimaging biomarkers with clinical features, as well as the prediction of clinical decline from the baseline measures. The successful applicant will be part of an interdisciplinary team supervised by Professor Lorina Naci, and based at the Global Brain Health Institute, at the Trinity College Institute of Neuroscience (TCIN), in the heart of Dublin. [S]he will receive top-tier training at the School of Psychology and TCIN, in cutting-edge translational neuroscience tools/methods (3T, 7T MRI) and research, and have international training and networking opportunities, including labs 3 visits in other world-leading universities (Edinburgh, Oxford, Cambridge (UK), UCSF (USA), INSERM (France)], access to unique aging/dementia large datasets, and shared resources. How to apply: Applicants should submit a full Curriculum Vitae to include the names and contact details of 2 referees (including email addresses), to:- Lorina Naci PREVENTDEMENTIA@TCD.IE Please see the Job Description below for this position. Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. We welcome applications from all individuals, including those with disabilities, those who have followed non-traditional career paths, and those who have taken time out for reasons such as family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants, including those who have been displaced due to conflict or war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier university. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and hold an Athena Swan Silver Award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and across the higher education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work–life balance and creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039516": {
      "title": "PhD Studentship",
      "description": "Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Post ID: 039516 Post Summary: St. James Hospital and the School of Nursing and Midwifery is offering one part-time funded scholarships for entry into the PhD programme March or September 2027. The scholarships are open to nurses and midwives who are not currently registered for a PhD, but who have a strong desire to discover new practices and knowledge through evidence based approaches. The Joint PhD Scholarship offers a pathway to nurses to conduct research that advances innovation in practice within St James Hospital and associated partners, for example to improve the integration of care, services or health systems. The Scholarships offer opportunities for suitably qualified nurses to pursue a Structured PhD which must align with one of with the nursing strategy pillars in St. James Hospital and of the Trinity Centre for Practice and Healthcare Innovation (TCPHI) research centre in the School of Nursing and Midwifery, Trinity College Dublin offers leading expertise: https://nursingmidwifery.tcd.ie/research/research-themes/index.php Areas of subject focus • Integrated Care • Workforce and People • Digital & Data • Chronic Illness • Cancer care & survivorship • Evidence-based nursing practice, Research & Innovation These are suggested areas, but the list is not exhaustive. Other fields of study that seek to enhance health outcomes for service users of St James Hospital will be considered. Post status: One six year part-time PhD scholarship How to apply: Applications for the part-time Scholarships should be submitted via the following link: https://www.tcd.ie/courses/postgraduate/postgraduate-research/health-sciences/school-ofnursing-and-midwifery/ The following documentation must be attached to the application: • Cover letter and CV • Personal statement including career plan (500 words) • Two letters of reference (academic) and one from clinical area (e.g. Assistant Director of Nursing) • Research proposal of approx. 3,000 words, to include specifically the following elements: - Abstract (300 words, approx.) - Background context and rationale for the research - Aims, objectives and central research questions - Methodology including details of any specialist knowledge/data required - Relevant ethical issues - Milestones and deliverables (Gantt chart) - Where the research fits within the School and College research strategies - Dissemination plans and potential impact The applicant will work with a potential supervisor from TCPHI to develop a robust Research Proposal to be submitted as part of the application process. Trinity College Dublin will charge an application fee. You may receive notification following your application to college that your application to be a PhD student has been accepted. This is a separate process from your application for the Scholarship. You will be notified of the outcome of your Scholarship application separately. Please see the Job Description below for this position. Application and Informal enquiries about this post should be made to Snehal Prabhukeluskar, Assistant Director of Evidence Based Nursing Research & Innovation, E: SPrabhukeluskar@stjames.ie TCD contact: Professor Anne-Marie Brady Director of Trinity Centre for Practice & Healthcare Innovation, E: abrady4@tcd.ie Please include the Competition ID number in the subject heading. Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. We welcome applications from all individuals, including those with disabilities, those who have followed non-traditional career paths, and those who have taken time out for reasons such as family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants, including those who have been displaced due to conflict or war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier university. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and hold an Athena Swan Silver Award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and across the higher education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work–life balance and creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039524": {
      "title": "Practice Manager College Health Service Trinity College",
      "description": "Closing Date: 22/10/2026 Job Title : Practice Manager College Health Service Trinity College Pay Scale : Administrative 2 Closing Date : 22-Oct-2026 12:00 Contact Email : recruit@tcd.ie Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Post ID: 039524 Post Summary: The primary purpose of the role of the Practice Manager at College Health is to contribute to the dynamic development of the College Health Service, ensuring that it remains responsive to the evolving medical needs of the students and staff of the College. This involves aligning with and advancing the mission, vision and values articulated in the College’s Strategic Plan. The Practice Manager oversees crucial aspects of the service, enabling its efficient and effective operation, and supports the Director in pursuing strategic priorities. The role’s context is shaped by the College Health Service’s commitment to providing modern, innovative, and inclusive services that make a tangible difference to the Trinity community. Through partnerships, innovative approaches and a commitment to continuous improvement, the College Health Service aims to continue to be a leader in the delivery of clinical care to students, and a flag bearer for College Health services across the Island, and beyond. Post status: Specific Purpose Contract Your employment is for the specific purpose of filling an Administrative 2 vacancy on a temporary basis pending the return of the postholder from maternity leave. Employment is not offered on an indefinite duration basis as the original postholder is expected to return. If the postholder does not return to work the post will be filled through open competition. The Unfair Dismissals Acts, 1977-2015 shall not apply to your dismissal consisting only of the cessation of the said purpose. Hours of Post: 35 hours per week Salary: This appointment will be made on the Administrative 2 Salary Scale (€60,185 - €70,718 per annum) at a point in line with current Government pay policy. monthly/weekly payscales. (tcd.ie) How to apply: To assist the selection process, applicants should submit a Curriculum Vitae and a Cover Letter (maximum one A4 page) that specifically addresses the following points: Clearly outline relevant experience and how this experience has contributed to their knowledge. Demonstrate, through past examples, their ability to work on their own initiative and to resolve problems. Applicants who do not address these requirements in their cover letter will not be considered at the shortlisting stage. Closing Date for Applications: 22nd October 2026 at 12 (noon). Any applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system. Please see the Job Description below for this position. Informal enquiries about this post should be made to Hiring Manager, David McGrath, Director Of College Health Service, E: dmcgrat@tcd.ie Application queries about this post, please email the Recruitment Partner, Rachel Jones at recruit@tcd.ie and include the Competition ID number in the subject heading. Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. We welcome applications from all individuals, including those with disabilities, those who have followed non-traditional career paths, and those who have taken time out for reasons such as family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants, including those who have been displaced due to conflict or war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier university. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and hold an Athena Swan Silver Award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and across the higher education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work–life balance and creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039520": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Post Summary : We seek to appoint an experienced Research Assistant to join the GLOMoL research team in the Department of Sociology, Trinity College Dublin. GLOMoL is a European Research Council (ERC) funded project led by Professor Mark Ward. This programme of research is a country-comparative 'Global study of the macro- and individual-level causes and consequences of loneliness'. The appointment is a specific purpose contract, to commence on 1st December 2026 or shortly thereafter. This role is offered for an initial period of 12 months, with the possibility of extension subject to performance and the ongoing requirements of the project. The successful candidate will have completed a Masters’s degree (MA, MSc, or equivalent) in quantitative sociology, applied research, or related social science discipline. The successful candidate should also have knowledge of or an interest in ageing and the social determinants of health, including loneliness. Post status: Specific Purpose Contract, Full-Time Application Procedure Applicants should submit a Cover Letter, full Curriculum Vitae that includes the names and contact details of 2 referees (including email addresses), to: Name: Ms Lorraine Ivers Email Address: recruit.sociology.sssp@tcd.ie Please see the Job Description below for this position. Enquiries about this post should be made to Prof Mark Ward at wardm8@tcd.ie Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. We welcome applications from all individuals, including those with disabilities, those who have followed non-traditional career paths, and those who have taken time out for reasons such as family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants, including those who have been displaced due to conflict or war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier university. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and hold an Athena Swan Silver Award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and across the higher education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work–life balance and creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039557": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/11/2026 Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Post ID: 039557 Post Summary: The post holder will be part of a multidisciplinary research team, based at St James’ Hospital in Dublin, working on studies related to interventions (drug and on-drug interventions) for Alzheimer disease, Lewy body and other forms of dementia. Our studies use different methods including imaging, biomarkers and neuropsychological methods to assess healthy volunteers, carers and people with lived experience. The post-holder’s primary job will be coordinating the Lewy Academy programme for training healthcare professionals, producing a policy brief and a systems map of the outputs of EMERALD-Lewy and how they can be integrated into existing policy structures in Ireland. Programme’s goal is to improve the diagnosis, management and lived experience of people with Lewy Body Dementia in Ireland. To this effect, the post holder will work together with the Programme’s stakeholders consisting of clinicians, scientists, industry partners, research assistants, PhD students and people with lived experience of Lewy body dementia and carers to coordinate data collection and drive the work package’s research output. The Research Assistant will be part of Work Package 2 and Work Package 10. The goal of WP 2 is to develop the clinical research in Ireland for Lewy Body Dementia, including the upskilling of current labour force. The leads of the work package are Prof. Iracema Leroi form Trinity College Dublin and Prof. Suzanne Timmons from University College Cork. The goal of WP 10 is to understand the policy entry points and propose a potential model of implementation of EMERALD-Lewy’s research outputs. This package is led by Prof. Kim Nyugen Huong from University of Queensland. The post holder will specifically support WP2 and WP10 by coordinating the running and assessment of the Lewy Academy training programme and developing a policy map and brief for the entry points of the outputs for EMERALD-Lewy into public policy in Ireland. They will also interface with the existing EMERALD-Lewy research team to support other research outputs. Post status: Specific Purpose Contract , Full Time Salary: This appointment will be made on the RA Level 1 Pay Grade according to IUA Salary Scales at a point in line with current Government pay policy. How to apply: Applicants should submit a full Curriculum Vitae to include the names and contact details of 2 referees (including email addresses), to:- Name: Anusha Yasoda-Mohan Email Address: anusha.mohan@tcd.ie Closing Date: Monday 9th November 2026 at 12:00 noon (Irish Standard time) Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. We welcome applications from all individuals, including those with disabilities, those who have followed non-traditional career paths, and those who have taken time out for reasons such as family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants, including those who have been displaced due to conflict or war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier university. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and hold an Athena Swan Silver Award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and across the higher education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work–life balance and creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039537": {
      "title": "Research Assistant (Research Nurse or Allied Health Professional TILDA)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/11/2026 Job Title : Research Assistant (Research Nurse or Allied Health Professional TILDA) Pay Scale : Closing Date : 09-Nov-2026 12:00 Contact Email : shughes8@tcd.ie About Trinity: Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Trinity’s Current strategic place: Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Post Summary: The Irish Longitudinal Study on Ageing (TILDA) is seeking Research Assistants from nursing and allied health backgrounds who wish to contribute to a well-established study with a strong reputation for producing high-quality, policy-relevant research on ageing. This role offers health professionals the opportunity to combine their existing clinical skills with hands-on experience in one of Ireland’s largest and longest-running studies of ageing, while developing specialist research skills and retaining substantial face-to-face contact with older adults. Hours Of Post: 39 hours per week Contract: Specific Purpose Contract, Full Time Salary: Appointment will be made on the relevant HSE Staff Nurse pay scale at a point in line with Government Pay Policy [€38,166 to €54,956 per annum], appointment will be made no higher than point 5, €44,213 Closing Date: 9th November 2026 at 12:00 noon (Irish Standard time) How to Apply: Applicants should submit a full Curriculum Vitae (max 3 x A4 pages) and Cover Letter (max 1 x A4 page) to include the names and contact details of 2 referees (including email addresses), to: Name: Sarah Hughes Email Address: shughes8@tcd.ie Informal enquiries about this post should be made to Ms Mary O’Shea, Research Nurse Manager at moshea9@tcd.ie, Ms Judy Sweeney, Research Nurse Manager at jusweene@tcd.ie or Project Manager, Dr Ann Monaghan at ann.monaghan@tcd.ie. Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging . We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together . We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining . And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. Trinity welcomes applications from all individuals, including those applicants with disabilities, those who may have had non-traditional career paths, those who have taken time out for reasons including family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants including those whom have been displaced due to war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier University. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and we hold an Athena Swan Silver award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and in the Higher Education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work-life balance and to creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039555": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Analytical Chemistry",
      "description": "Closing Date: 06/11/2026 Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Post Summary: Our research group is investigating skin allergy related to medical devices. Continuous glucose-monitoring devices and insulin pump systems have become an essential component of the management of diabetes, a chronic disease that affects over 500 million people worldwide. As the prevalence of diabetes increases, the use of these devices is also expanding. These devices are affixed to the patient’s skin using an adhesive patch. Allergic contact dermatitis (ACD) to components of glucose-monitoring sensors and insulin pumps is increasingly problematic as disease management becomes dependent on wearable technologies. The objective of this project is to analyse the chemical composition of diabetic medical devices to understand what allergenic chemicals are present, focusing on emerging skin allergens. We expect to establish the identity and quantity of skin allergens present in devices currently used by patients. In line with best practice, we will take a dual approach and patch-test patients presenting with skin allergy to these devices to identify the clinically relevant allergens. This will allow us to counsel patients on the best strategy for avoiding skin allergy to their medical devices. Identification of skin allergens will also provide valuable information to the manufacturers of these devices to enable replacement of currently used chemicals with better, less toxic alternatives. Salary: Appointment will be made on the IUA researcher salary scale for postdoctoral researcher PD1 at a point in line with Government Pay Policy (€47,273 to €51,176 per annum). Appointment will be made no higher than point 4. How to apply: Applicants should submit a one-page cover letter and a full Curriculum Vitae to include the names and contact details of 2 referees (including email addresses), to: Name: Dr. Niamh O’Boyle Email Address: nioboyle@tcd.ie Please add ‘Application for CDSCHC postdoctoral fellowship’ in the email subject line. Closing Date: 12 noon (GMT) on 6th November 2026 Late applications will not be accepted. Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. We welcome applications from all individuals, including those with disabilities, those who have followed non-traditional career paths, and those who have taken time out for reasons such as family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants, including those who have been displaced due to conflict or war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier university. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and hold an Athena Swan Silver Award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and across the higher education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work–life balance and creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039500": {
      "title": "Race Equality Action Plan Project Officer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 27/10/2026 Job Title : Race Equality Action Plan Project Officer Pay Scale : Administrative 2 Closing Date : 27-Oct-2026 12:00 Contact Email : recruit@tcd.ie Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 The Race Equality Action Plan Project Officer will support and coordinate the implementation of the Trinity Race Equality Action Plan (2023-28) (REAP) in collaboration with the Equality, Diversity, and Inclusion (EDI) Office and the University REAP Implementation Oversight Group (UREAP IOG). This role is based in the EDI Office at Trinity College Dublin. The REAP details the key initiatives related to this work, which is is connected to the University Athena Swan Action Plan and the Higher Education Authority Anti-Racism Principles. This role will serve an important function in promoting race equality and fostering an inclusive, anti-racist culture at Trinity. Working closely with colleagues, students, and external partners the role will support anti-racism and related EDI initiatives, including organising and delivering tailored training, resources, and awareness initiatives to tight timelines. In addition, the role will provide essential administrative and project support to ensure race equality and EDI Office activities are impactful, timely, coordinated to a high standard, and aligned with Trinity’s strategic plan, values, and goals. Post Status: Fixed Term This appointment is offered for a fixed period, terminating on 30th September 2027 to support and coordinate the implementation of the Trinity Race Equality Action Plan (2023-2028) (REAP) in collaboration with the Trinity Equality, Diversity, and Inclusion Office (EDI) and the University REAP Implementation Oversight Group (UREAP-IOG).This role is not offered on a permanent basis, as it is required for a defined period to support the implementation of REAP, including the coordination, delivery and monitoring of key actions and initiatives under the Plan. The requirement for the role beyond the contract end-date will be subject to review, having regard to the remaining implementation requirements of REAP, associated resourcing requirements and the availability of funding Hours of Post: 35 hours per week Salary: Appointment will be made on the Administrative Officer 2 (€60,185 - €70,718 per annum) and in accordance with the Department of Finance guidelines.” https://www.tcd.ie/hr/employee-services/pay-scales/ How to Apply : To assist the selection process, applicants should submit a Curriculum Vitae and a Cover Letter (1x A4 page) that specifically address the following points in their application. Applicants should clearly address their experience and skills relevant to this role and how they obtained their knowledge in their application. Applicants should illustrate, through specific past example(s), their ability to work on their own initiative, to coordinate projects to a required timeline and standard of work, and to resolve problems. Applicant should communicate their ability to engage effectively with the focus of the role, in particular EDI and race equality themes. Closing Date: 12 noon (Irish Standard Time) 27th October 2026 Application will only be accepted by applying online through the Trinity Jobs Portal http://jobs.tcd.ie Applications must be made by the date and time specified. Please allow enough time to make your application . Any applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system Late Applications will not be accepted . Please see the Job Description below for this position. Informal enquiries about this post should be made to Mr Joel McKeever, Equality Officer, at equality@tcd.ie. Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. Trinity welcomes applications from all individuals, including those applicants with disabilities, those who may have had non-traditional career paths, those who have taken time out for reasons including family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants including those who have been displaced due to war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier University. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and we hold an Athena Swan Silver award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and in the Higher Education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work-life balance and to creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039553": {
      "title": "Associate Professor in Digital Health, Artificial Intelligence and Society",
      "description": "Closing Date: 12/11/2026 Job Title : Associate Professor in Digital Health, Artificial Intelligence and Society Pay Scale : Associate Professor Closing Date : 12-Nov-2026 12:00 Contact Email : recruit@tcd.ie Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Post ID: 039553 Post Summary: The School of Medicine seeks to appoint a 1.0 FTE Associate Professor in Digital Health, Artificial Intelligence and Society to provide academic leadership at a moment of profound technological transformation in healthcare and higher education. This is a strategic joint appointment between the School of Medicine and the University. The postholder will devote approximately half of their activity to establishing institutional capability in Digital Health, AI and Connected Health within medicine. The other half will be dedicated to a new university-wide initiative, currently in development, that will explore how artificial intelligence may fundamentally reshape learning, universities, knowledge creation and professional formation. The postholder will be expected to play a formative role in shaping this initiative from its earliest stages. The role will also explore how artificial intelligence reshapes learning, research, and higher education and contribute to Trinity's broader exploration of the future of education, scholarship and public trust in an AI-enabled world. Working across disciplines, the postholder will build educational programmes, research collaborations, international partnerships and experimental initiatives that strengthen digital capability, promote critical inquiry, and ensure that technological innovation remains firmly centred on human values. This appointment recognises that digital transformation represents one of the defining changes facing healthcare and all of modern society. Future clinicians and other professionals must understand not only how to use digital technologies and artificial intelligence, but also how to evaluate them critically, understand their limitations, appreciate their ethical implications and integrate them safely into practice. The successful candidate will contribute to teaching, curriculum development, educational scholarship, research and external engagement, helping position Trinity College Dublin as a national leader in digital health education and AI literacy. Post status: Specific Purpose Contract The specific purpose of this employment is to establish, develop and expand institutional capability in Digital Health, Artificial Intelligence and Connected Health within the School of Medicine and across Trinity College Dublin, through the delivery of a defined programme of strategic projects. This employment is not offered on an indefinite duration basis as the appointment is required to deliver a defined, specific and time-limited, programme to establish a university-wide ecosystem for responsible AI innovation in medicine, education and research. When the specific programme of work is completed, there will no longer be a need for the role. Hours of Post: Hours of work for academic staff are those as prescribed under Public Service Agreements. For further information please follow this link: http://www.tcd.ie/hr/assets/pdf/academic-hours-public-service-agreement.pdf Salary: Appointment will be made on Associate Professor Salary Scale commensurate with qualifications / experience and in line with Public Sector Pay Policy [€93,914 - €121,273 per annum] https://www.tcd.ie/hr/assets/pdf/monthly-academic.pdf How to apply : Applicants should submit: Cover Letter (maximum one page) Curriculum Vitae Teaching Statement (maximum two pages) Research and Scholarship Statement (maximum two pages) Vision Statement outlining how they would contribute to building capacity in Connected Health, Digital Health and AI Literacy within Trinity College Dublin (maximum two pages) Candidates who do not submit the above application documents will not be considered for shortlisting. Any applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system. Please see the Job Description below for this position . Informal enquiries about this post should be made to Colin Doherty, Professor Consultant, Clinical Medicine, E: CDOHERT@tcd.ie Application queries about this post, please email Katie Doran, Recruitment Partner at E recruit@tcd.ie and include the Competition ID number in the subject heading. Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. We welcome applications from all individuals, including those with disabilities, those who have followed non-traditional career paths, and those who have taken time out for reasons such as family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants, including those who have been displaced due to conflict or war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier university. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and hold an Athena Swan Silver Award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and across the higher education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work–life balance and creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039488": {
      "title": "Craft Gardener",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/10/2026 Job Title : Craft Gardener Pay Scale : Craftperson Closing Date : 30-Oct-2026 12:00 Contact Email : Recruit@tcd.ie About Trinity: Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Post Summary: A vacancy exists for the permanent position of a fully qualified Craft Gardener in the Campus Maintenance Team within the Campus Services section of the Estates & Facilities Department. A minimum of three-year post qualification and experience in a similar role is required. The Craft Gardener will primarily be assigned to the Craft Gardening Team which is a sub team of the larger Campus Maintenance Team, but they will work across the full Campus Maintenance service. The Craft Gardener in will have dedicated area of responsibility, but teamwork is essential across the team to ensure knowledge is shared and each member of the team and can support and provide cover for team members when necessary. The Craft Gardener in will report to the Chargehand Craft Gardener, who in turn reports to the Team Leader who in turn reports into the Campus & Grounds Maintenance Manager who in turn reports to the Campus Services Manager. Post status: Permanent Hours of Post: 39 hours per week Salary: This appointment will be made on the Fortnightly Craftperson Salary Scale (€1,783.10 - €1,872.47 per fortnight) at a point in line with current Government pay policy. monthly/weekly payscales. (tcd.ie) How to apply: In order to assist the selection process, applicants should submit a Curriculum Vitae and a Cover Letter (1x A4 page) that specifically address the following points in their application. Applicants should clearly address their experience and how they obtained their knowledge in their application. Illustrate, through past example, their ability to work on their own initiative and resolve problems. Applicants who do not address the application requirements above in their cover letter will not be considered at the short list stage. Applications must be submitted by the closing date specified through eRecruitment. Any Applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system. Please see the Job Description below for this position. Informal enquiries about this post should be made to John Parnell Application queries about this post, please email Aisling Bowden at recruit@tcd.ie and include the Competition ID number in the subject heading. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. Trinity welcomes applications from all individuals, including those applicants with disabilities, those who may have had non-traditional career paths, those who have taken time out for reasons including family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants including those whom have been displaced due to war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier University. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and we hold an Athena Swan Silver award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and in the Higher Education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work-life balance and to creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039563": {
      "title": "Demonstrator in Anatomy",
      "description": "Closing Date: 16/11/2026 Job Title : Demonstrator in Anatomy Pay Scale : Demonstrator Merged Closing Date : 16-Nov-2026 12:00 Contact Email : recruit@tcd.ie Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Post ID: 039563 Post Summary : The School of Medicine seeks to appoint a Demonstrator in Anatomy for 12 months. The successful candidate will provide teaching to students of anatomy at TCD. The post holder will assist the discipline in curricular development activities including vertical integration, blended learning and exam preparation. Research may also be conducted in one of the areas of expertise of the discipline including human anatomy, immunology, cancer, sports and exercise medicine, medical education, and neuroscience. Previous experience in cadaveric anatomy is essential. Previous experience in research and undergraduate anatomy teaching and radiology is highly desirable. The post holder must have an excellent command of the English language and good interpersonal, communication, IT and presentation skills. The post offers an opportunity to expand one’s research portfolio and expertise in anatomy to prepare for a career in medicine, industry, or academia. Post status: 1 Year Fixed-Term Contract This post is not being offered on a permanent basis as it is designated as a training position. The nature of the role is intended as an opportunity to expand one’s research portfolio and expertise in Anatomy to prepare for a career in the Medicine Industry/Academia. As such, the role is not intended for long-term employment beyond the duration of such training. Hours of Post: 39 hours per week Salary: This appointment will be made on the Demonstrator Merged Scale (€41,586 - €45,128 per annum) at a point in line with current Government pay policy. monthly/weekly payscales. (tcd.ie) How to apply: Candidates should submit: Cover Letter (1x A4 page) A comprehensive curriculum vitae, including a full list of publications The names and contact details (i.e. addresses, e-mail, etc.) of three referees A comprehensive research plan (maximum 2 pages) and a teaching statement (maximum 2 pages) Please Note: Applicants who do not submit the above will not be considered at the short list stage Any applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system. Please see the Job Description below for this position. Informal enquiries about this post should be made to Denis Barry, Head of Discipline, at E: debarry@tcd.ie Application queries about this post, please email Katie Doran, Recruitment Partner at E: recruit@tcd.ie and include the Competition ID number in the subject heading. Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. We welcome applications from all individuals, including those with disabilities, those who have followed non-traditional career paths, and those who have taken time out for reasons such as family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants, including those who have been displaced due to conflict or war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier university. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and hold an Athena Swan Silver Award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and across the higher education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work–life balance and creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039410": {
      "title": "Dual Degree Programme Officer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/10/2026 Job Title : Dual Degree Programme Officer Pay Scale : Administrative 2 Closing Date : 30-Oct-2026 12:00 Contact Email : recruit@tcd.ie Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 The role holder is the first point of contact for staff, students and external partners on Dual Degree programmes and will be part of a small team of administrative staff who provide services to management, colleagues (internal and external) and students within the remit of the Undergraduate Common Architecture Office (UCAO). The UCAO works closely with the Associate Dean for the Undergraduate Common Architecture (ADUCA); the Director of Dual Degree Programmes (DDP); a number of units across College; and partner institutions to ensure that the needs of the students and staff in Dual Degree programmes, Trinity Joint Honours and shared elements of the Common Architecture are met. The role reports to the Manager of the Undergraduate Common Architecture Office. The Dual Degree Programme between Trinity College Dublin and Columbia University offers students an international undergraduate experience spanning two continents, in cosmopolitan cities, that allows students to enhance their global knowledge, language skills and communication abilities. The programme draws upon elements both traditional and innovative, combining the academic rigour of two world-renowned universities with an attention to the roles that social and cultural traditions play in a student’s intellectual formation. This is a full-time role in Ireland. We require that you hold immigration permission that allows full-time employment in this role. For this position we will not be able to support new employment permit applications. Post Status: Specific Purpose The specific purpose of filling the Dual Degree Programme Officer vacancy on a temporary basis pending the return of the postholder from maternity leave. Employment is not offered on an indefinite duration basis as the original postholder is expected to return. Hours of Post: 35 hours per week Salary: Appointment will be made on the Administrative Officer 2 (€60,185 - €70,718 per annum) and in accordance with the Department of Finance guidelines.” https://www.tcd.ie/hr/employee-services/pay-scales/ How to Apply : In order to assist the selection process, applicants should submit a Curriculum Vitae and a Cover Letter (1x A4 page) that specifically address the following points in their application. Applicants should clearly address this experience and how they obtained their knowledge in their application. An understanding of the unique challenges that face students embarking on international educational experiences. The applicant should give examples of involvement in supporting international students and what the applicant learned from the process. Illustrate, through past example, their ability to work on their own initiative and resolve problems. Closing Date: 12 noon (Irish Standard Time) 25th September 2026 Application will only be accepted by applying online through the Trinity Jobs Portal http://jobs.tcd.ie Applications must be made by the date and time specified. Please allow enough time to make your application . Any applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system Late Applications will not be accepted . Please see the Job Description below for this position. Informal enquiries about this post should be made to mcpeakm@tcd.ie Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. Trinity welcomes applications from all individuals, including those applicants with disabilities, those who may have had non-traditional career paths, those who have taken time out for reasons including family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants including those who have been displaced due to war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier University. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and we hold an Athena Swan Silver award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and in the Higher Education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work-life balance and to creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039535": {
      "title": "Environmental Operations Manager",
      "description": "Closing Date: 26/10/2026 Job Title : Environmental Operations Manager Pay Scale : Administrative 2 Closing Date : 26-Oct-2026 12:00 Contact Email : recruit@tcd.ie Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Post ID: 039535 Post Summary: A vacancy exists for an experienced professional in the position of Environmental Operations Manager within the Estates and Facilities (E&F) Department of Trinity College Dublin. The purpose of the role is to plan and manage the provision of high-quality environmental and sustainability services across all campuses for the students, staff and visitors of Trinity College Dublin and its wider Estate. The Environmental Operations Manager will plan, develop, implement, and manage a demonstrably high-quality environmental and sustainability service that supports the organisational requirements of the University across all its campuses. The role will take responsibility for the leadership and management of the experienced team of approximately 2 staff and provide the necessary cohesive oversight and management of supplementary contracted waste, external drainage and pest control service provisions. Post status: Permanent Contract Hours of Post: 35 hours per week Salary: This appointment will be made on the Administrative 2 Salary Scale ( €60,185 - €70,718 per annum) at a point in line with current Government pay policy. monthly/weekly payscales. (tcd.ie) How to apply: To assist the selection process, applicants should submit a Curriculum Vitae and a Cover Letter (maximum one A4 page) that specifically addresses the following points: Clearly outline relevant experience and how this experience has contributed to their knowledge. Demonstrate, through past examples, their ability to work on their own initiative and to resolve problems. Application closing date: 26th October - 12pm noon Applicants who do not address these requirements in their cover letter will not be considered at the shortlisting stage. Any applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system. Please see the Job Description below for this position. Informal enquiries about this post should be made to Hiring Manager David Marley, Campus Service Manager, E: marleyd@tcd.ie Application queries about this post, please email the Recruitment Partner, Rachel Jones at rajones@tcd.ie and include the Competition ID number in the subject heading. Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. We welcome applications from all individuals, including those with disabilities, those who have followed non-traditional career paths, and those who have taken time out for reasons such as family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants, including those who have been displaced due to conflict or war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier university. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and hold an Athena Swan Silver Award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and across the higher education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work–life balance and creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039566": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Post ID: 039566 Post Summary :- The Complex Software Lab at Trinity College Dublin is seeking to appoint a Research Assistant to undertake research on the testing, assurance and conformity assessment of Artificial Intelligence systems. As AI systems are increasingly deployed in regulated and high-impact settings, there is a growing need for systematic approaches for determining whether such systems satisfy technical, regulatory and organisational requirements. This includes establishing what constitutes adequate evidence that an AI system has been appropriately tested and assessing properties such as accuracy, robustness, security, fairness, traceability, transparency and reliability. The project will investigate how established approaches from software testing, AI evaluation, audit and assurance can be adapted to support the assessment of AI systems. Particular attention will be given to the relationship between technical testing and the evidence required to support AI assurance and conformity assessment, including in the context of European AI regulation. The successful candidate will work in an interdisciplinary environment at the intersection of software engineering, artificial intelligence, digital forensics, audit and regulation. The research will combine analysis of existing practices and standards with empirical evaluation of AI systems and the development of practical testing and assurance methodologies. Post status: Specific Purpose Contract Hours of Post: TBD, full-time equivalent of 39 hours per week Salary: Appointment will be made on the Research Ireland Team Member Salary Scale for Research Assistants at a point in line with Government Pay Policy [€34,130 to €44,311 per annum]. The specific salary point will be determined by qualifications and experience, and in accordance with RI and TCD guidelines. Application Procedure Applicants should submit: • A full Curriculum Vitae (CV), including a list of publications and the names and contact details of two referees. • A cover letter detailing their experience and motivation for applying. Submit applications to: Name: Dr. Anthony Ventresque Email: anthony.ventresque@tcd.ie Please see the Job Description below for this position. Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. We welcome applications from all individuals, including those with disabilities, those who have followed non-traditional career paths, and those who have taken time out for reasons such as family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants, including those who have been displaced due to conflict or war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier university. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and hold an Athena Swan Silver Award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and across the higher education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work–life balance and creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039547": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Sociology",
      "description": "Closing Date: 20/11/2026 Job Title : Assistant Professor in Sociology Pay Scale : Lecturer Merged Closing Date : 20-Nov-2026 12:00 Contact Email : recruit@tcd.ie About Trinity: Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Trinity College Dublin- an introduction. Post Summary The Department of Sociology, School of Social Sciences and Philosophy, invites applications for the position of Assistant Professor in Sociology (Quantitative Sociology), commencing on 3 August 2027. The Department seeks applications from academics who will contribute significantly to research and teaching excellence in one or more of the Department’s research streams: Migration, Identities and Diversity; Power, Conflict and Resistance; Comparative Inequalities, Education and Families; Digitalisation, AI and Social Networks; and Environment and Society (https://www.tcd.ie/sociology/research/). The appointee will teach and supervise at both undergraduate and postgraduate levels, engage in public and policy debates, and collaborate with other departments and external research partners. Candidates must have a strong quantitative research orientation, evidenced by their publication record and research agenda. Candidates must have expertise in advanced quantitative approaches, such as, but not limited to, causal inference, longitudinal data analysis, cross-nationally comparative designs, or computational social science methods. Candidates should demonstrate methodological expertise that is both rigorous and innovative. Applicants must hold a PhD in Sociology or a closely related discipline and, relative to career stage, show evidence of high-impact scholarship in international, peer-reviewed journals as well as experience in teaching and supervision. Department/Faculty: School of Social Sciences and Philosophy, Department of Sociology Location: Trinity College Dublin, The University of Dublin, College Green, Dublin 2, Ireland Reports to: Head of Discipline and Head of School Post Status: 5 Year Fixed Term Tenure-Track Salary : Appointment will be made on Assistant Professor Merged Salary Scale (109BN) commensurate with qualifications / experience and in line with Public Sector Pay Policy (€43,859 - €104,618 per annum) https://www.tcd.ie/hr/assets/pdf/monthly-academic.pdf Hours of Work : Hours of work for academic staff are those as prescribed under Public Service Agreements. For further information please follow the link below: http://www.tcd.ie/hr/assets/pdf/academic-hours-public-service-agreement.pdf Closing Date: 12 Noon (Irish Standard Time), 20 November 2026 Application Information Candidates must submit the following: 1. Cover letter – 2 x A4 pages maximum 2. Full curriculum vitae to include the names and contact details of 3 referees, 3. Research statement (summarising current research and future research plans) – 2 x A4 pages maximum 4. Teaching statement (summarising teaching experience and approach – 2 x A4 pages maximum) Please Note: Candidates who do not submit the requested information will not be considered for shortlisting. Candidates should note that the interview process for this appointment will include the delivery of a presentation as well as an interview. Applications will only be accepted by applying online through the Trinity Jobs Portal http://jobs.tcd.ie Informal enquiries about this post please contact Associate Professor Dr Yekaterina Chzhen, Acting Head of the Department of Sociology, at chzheny@tcd.ie Application queries about this post, please email the Recruitment Partner at E: recruit@tcd.ie and include the Competition ID number in the subject heading. Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. Trinity welcomes applications from all individuals, including those applicants with disabilities, those who may have had non-traditional career paths, those who have taken time out for reasons including family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants including those who have been displaced due to war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier University. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and we hold an Athena Swan Silver award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and in the Higher Education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work-life balance and to creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039344": {
      "title": "Business Development Manager",
      "description": "Closing Date: 02/11/2026 Job Title : Business Development Manager Pay Scale : Administrative 1 Closing Date : 02-Nov-2026 12:00 Contact Email : recruit@tcd.ie Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Trinity College Dublin seeks to appoint a Business Development Manager to support the establishment of its new Research Ireland research centre in quantum information, science and technology, Rinn Quantum. The centre brings together researchers across academia and industry to develop a responsible and thriving quantum ecosystem which will enable the cultivation of a diverse and sustainable talent pipeline. To support its establishment and growth, Rinn Quantum is seeking a dynamic and strategic Business Development Manager to expand partnerships, drive commercialisation opportunities, and strengthen the Centre’s position as a national and international leader in quantum science, technology and innovation. The Business Development Manager will play a pivotal role in connecting Rinn Quantum’s research capabilities with industry, government, and funding bodies. This position requires a blend of strategic thinking, relationship‑building, and commercial acumen, with the ability to navigate both academic and industry environments. The successful candidate will identify new opportunities, develop strategic partnerships, support technology transfer, and contribute to the Centre’s long‑term sustainability and impact. The successful candidate must assume a higher level of leadership and responsibility with respect to ownership and delivery of the industry strategy ensuring cohesion through the team, shared goals and target definition for monitoring of performance. Post Status: Specific Purpose This role is for the specific purpose of leading and supporting the development, growth, and delivery of the Rinn Quantum Research Centre’s business development and industry engagement activities. This includes identifying and securing new partnership opportunities, supporting the commercialisation of research outputs, and contributing to the expansion of the Centre’s collaborative and funding portfolio across its thematic research areas. Employment is not offered on an indefinite duration basis, as all activities are directly linked to the scope and duration of the Rinn Quantum Research Centre and are expected to come to an end. The role is dependent on the continued requirement for specialised business development and external engagement support within the Rinn Quantum Research Centre. Hours of Post: 35 hours per week Salary: Appointment will be made on the Administrative Officer 1 (€71,651- €113,454 per annum) and in accordance with the Department of Finance guidelines.” https://www.tcd.ie/hr/employee-services/pay-scales/ How to Apply : In order to assist the selection process, applicants should submit a curriculum vitae and a cover letter (1x A4 page) that specifically addresses the following points in their application. Outline how their experience aligns with the role’s responsibilities. The applicant’s vision for industrial engagement in a quantum research context. Closing Date: 12 noon (Irish Standard Time) 2nd November 2026 Application will only be accepted by applying online through the Trinity Jobs Portal https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_details_form.jobspec?p_id=039298 Applications must be made by the date and time specified. Please allow enough time to make your application . Any applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system Late Applications will not be accepted . Please see the Job Description below for this position. Interviews will take place in November 2026 Informal enquiries on the role can be addressed to Professor John Goold, Centre Co-Director, School of Physics at GOOLDJ@tcd.ie. Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. Trinity welcomes applications from all individuals, including those applicants with disabilities, those who may have had non-traditional career paths, those who have taken time out for reasons including family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants including those who have been displaced due to war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier University. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and we hold an Athena Swan Silver award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and in the Higher Education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work-life balance and to creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039528": {
      "title": "Facility Attendant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 02/11/2026 Job Title : Facility Attendant Pay Scale : Attendant Merged Closing Date : 02-Nov-2026 12:00 Contact Email : recruit@tcd.ie About Trinity: Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Post Summary The attendant staff provide a range of specific services [details below] within College buildings both on and off Campus, Attendants can be appointed to a regular post or be assigned as part of the Supernumerary team. Supernumerary team cover all shifts in Attendant Services for absences, annual leave, sick leave etc., for static Attendants between the hours of 7am and 11pm, details below. Post status: Specific Purpose Contract The specific purpose of filling this vacancy on a temporary basis pending the return of the postholder from authorised leave. Hours of Post: This is a specific purpose contract working between buildings in Premises Area 6 Rotating roster with weekly early and late shifts Working hours between 7am and 10pm , Monday to Friday Required to work every second Saturday , 7am to 1pm Salary: This appointment will be made on the Fortnightly Merged Attendant Salary Scale (€ 1,368.75 - € 1,540.57 per fortnight) at a point in line with current Government pay policy. monthly/weekly payscales. (tcd.ie) How to apply: In order to assist the selection process, applicants should submit a Curriculum Vitae and a Cover Letter (1x A4 page) that specifically address the following points in their application. Applicants should clearly address their experience and how they obtained their knowledge in their application. Illustrate, through past example, their ability to work on their own initiative and resolve problems. Applicants who do not address the application requirements above in their cover letter will not be considered at the short list stage. Applications must be submitted by the closing date specified through eRecruitment. Any Applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system. Please see the Job Description below for this position. Informal enquiries about this post should be made to Jane Flanagan Application queries about this post, please email the Recruitment Partner at recruit@tcd.ie and include the Competition ID number in the subject heading. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. Trinity welcomes applications from all individuals, including those applicants with disabilities, those who may have had non-traditional career paths, those who have taken time out for reasons including family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants including those whom have been displaced due to war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier University. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and we hold an Athena Swan Silver award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and in the Higher Education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work-life balance and to creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039463": {
      "title": "Library Digital Communications Officer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 02/11/2026 Job Title : Library Digital Communications Officer Pay Scale : Higher Library Assistant (Exec 1) Closing Date : 02-Nov-2026 12:00 Contact Email : recruit@tcd.ie Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Post ID: 039463 Post Summary :- This is a varied and challenging role in a fast-paced environment based in the Library of Trinity College Dublin. The appointed candidate will be responsible for the promotion of the Library user experience and resources, as well as a range of activities and events across digital communications, social media platforms, traditional channels and the Library website. The appointee will design and manage the production of promotional material for targeted campaigns, activities and events for Library readers, the internal Trinity community and external audiences and public. They include Library displays, announcements, exhibitions, talks, workshops and seminars forming part of the Library’s core activities and outreach programme. The appointee will also assist with the event management and IT requirements for a range of Library activities hosted in online and physical settings. Post status: Permanent Contract Hours of Post: 35 hours per week Salary: This appointment will be made on the Higher Library Assistant (Exec 1) Salary Scale ( €56,677 - €74,738 per annum) at a point in line with current Government pay policy. monthly/weekly payscales. (tcd.ie) How to apply: To assist the selection process, applicants should submit a Curriculum Vitae and a Cover Letter (maximum one A4 page) that specifically addresses the following points: Clearly outline relevant experience and how this experience has contributed to their knowledge. Demonstrate, through past examples, their ability to work on their own initiative and to resolve problems. Applicants who do not address these requirements in their cover letter will not be considered at the shortlisting stage. Any applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system. Please see the Job Description below for this position. Informal enquiries about this post should be made to Caoimhe Ni Lochlainn, Head Of Library Communications, E: NILOCHLC@tcd.ie Application queries about this post, please email Andrea at recruit@tcd.ie and include the Competition ID number in the subject heading. Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. We welcome applications from all individuals, including those with disabilities, those who have followed non-traditional career paths, and those who have taken time out for reasons such as family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants, including those who have been displaced due to conflict or war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier university. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and hold an Athena Swan Silver Award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and across the higher education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work–life balance and creating a family-friendly working environment."
