    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039575": {
      "title": "PhD Studentship",
      "description": "Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Post Summary: A 4 year Postgraduate Research Studentship is available to new entrants on the full-time PhD register for entry in September each year. Applicants must have received a minimum of 2.1 Honours degree to be eligible for the award. The postgraduate studentship aims to support and develop gifted research students and is awarded competitively on academic merit. It fully covers EU or non-EU fees and provides an annual maintenance of €25,000. Applications investigating topics within national strategies and/or the School research agenda are particularly welcome. You may select your own topic but you can also choose from the following school expertise: Research Centres: Trinity Centre for Maternal Care Research (TCMCR) Trinity Research in Childhood Centre (TRiCC) Trinity Centre for Ageing and Intellectual Disabilities (TCAID) Trinity Centre for Practice and Healthcare Innovation (TCPHI) Thematic areas: Mental Health and Recovery Older persons health & well-being How to apply: Candidate should submit their application, along with a full Curriculum Vitae and a word version of a 3000 word maximum research proposal, Online at the following link: http://www.tcd.ie/courses/postgraduate/research/schools/nursing-midwifery.php Please see the Job Description below for this position. If you have any application queries, please contact Marie Pierre Lavergne: lavergnm@tcd.ie Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. We welcome applications from all individuals, including those with disabilities, those who have followed non-traditional career paths, and those who have taken time out for reasons such as family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants, including those who have been displaced due to conflict or war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier university. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and hold an Athena Swan Silver Award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and across the higher education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work–life balance and creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039519": {
      "title": "Research Funding Specialist (International)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 04/11/2026 Job Title : Research Funding Specialist (International) Pay Scale : Administrative 2 Closing Date : 04-Nov-2026 12:00 Contact Email : recruiit@tcd.ie Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Applications are invited for the post of Research Funding Specialist within the Research Development Office (RDO). The post will provide support to the RDO’s international research funding activities, working as part of the central team of Research Funding Specialists and alongside research support colleagues based in Schools and Centres. The role will support academic researchers and research groups in accessing international research funding opportunities through the coordination and delivery of funding information, application support, workshops, interview preparation and associated administrative processes. Reporting to the Team Lead, the post holder will work closely with a Senior Research Funding Specialist responsible for the international funding portfolio. The role will provide support in relation to appropriate international funding programmes, including the coordination of funding bulletins and information, standard application guidance, management of records relating to grant applications, workshops and other researcher-support activities. Post Status: Specific Purpose The specific purpose of this employment is to provide partial cover for a vacancy on a temporary basis pending the return of the postholder from maternity leave. Employment is not offered on an indefinite duration basis as the original postholder is expected to return. If the postholder does not return to work the post will be filled through open competition. Hours of Post: 35 hours per week Salary: Appointment will be made on the Administrative Officer 2 (€60,185- €70,718 per annum) and in accordance with the Department of Finance guidelines.” https://www.tcd.ie/hr/employee-services/pay-scales/ How to Apply : To assist the selection process, applicants should submit a Cover Letter (maximum 1 x A4 page) and a Curriculum Vitae that specifically addresses how their experience meets the requirements for this job. Please Note: Candidates who do not address the application requirements above in their cover letter will not be considered at the short list stage. Candidates should note that the interview process for this appointment will include the delivery of a presentation. Closing Date: 12 noon (Irish Standard Time) 4th November 2026 Application will only be accepted by applying online through the Trinity Jobs Portal Applications must be made by the date and time specified. Please allow enough time to make your application . Any applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system Late Applications will not be accepted . Please see the Job Description below for this position. Interviews will be held the week of 23rd November 2026 Informal enquiries on the role can be addressed to Sarah Nangle sarah.nangle@tcd.ie Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. Trinity welcomes applications from all individuals, including those applicants with disabilities, those who may have had non-traditional career paths, those who have taken time out for reasons including family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants including those who have been displaced due to war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier University. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and we hold an Athena Swan Silver award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and in the Higher Education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work-life balance and to creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039552": {
      "title": "Senior Category Manager (Capital Works)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 12/11/2026 Job Title : Senior Category Manager (Capital Works) Pay Scale : Senior Administrative 3 Closing Date : 12-Nov-2026 12:00 Contact Email : recruit@tcd.ie Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Thrive: Trinity College Dublin's Strategic Plan 2025 - 2030 Post Summary: Trinity College Dublin, the University of Dublin, is seeking to appoint a Senior Category Manager (Capital Programme) within the Capital Programme Office (CPO). The role is a key procurement leadership position supporting the implementation of Trinity's strategic development agenda, including delivery of the Capital Masterplan and associated multi-year capital investment programme. Reporting to the Director of the Capital Programme Office, the postholder will lead the development, establishment and embedding of the Capital Programme Procurement function within Trinity, providing strategic procurement leadership, commercial governance and category management across a significant portfolio of capital projects. The role will be responsible for developing procurement frameworks, governance arrangements, commercial management processes and organisational capability that support both programme delivery and the longer-term transition of the function to a mature business-as-usual operating model. This role will lead Capital Programme Procurement, providing both strategic procurement leadership and hands-on delivery across the Capital Works category. The successful candidate will bring extensive experience in the Capital Works Management Framework (CWMF), Irish and EU public procurement regulations, and buyer-side eTenders operations, with a proven ability to develop compliant procurement strategies, address delivery challenges, and support key programme and project milestones. Working collaboratively with the Capital Programme Office and key stakeholders across Estates & Facilities, the Project Management Office, and programme delivery teams, the post holder will ensure procurement activities align with organisational objectives, governance requirements, and successful project outcomes. Post status: 5 Year Fixed-Term Contract You will be responsible for the delivery and governance of large-scale construction and transformation projects and for leading procurement activities across the Capital Works category. This role is not offered on a permanent basis as it is required for a defined five-year implementation phase of Trinity's strategic development and capital master planning programme. During this period, the role will support the delivery of the University's significant capital programme and establish and embed the Capital Programme Procurement function, including associated procurement frameworks, governance arrangements and commercial management processes. The contract is fixed to the term above as this reflects the anticipated timeframe required to deliver these objectives and transition the function from establishment to business-as-usual operation. The requirement for the role beyond this period will be reviewed having regard to the scale and nature of the remaining capital programme and the University's longer-term operational and resourcing requirements. Hours of Post: 35 hours per week Salary: This appointment will be made on the Senior Administrative 3 Salary Scale (€101,847 - €131,519) per annum) at a point in line with current Government pay policy. monthly/weekly payscales. (tcd.ie) How to apply: To assist the selection process, applicants must submit the following: • A comprehensive Curriculum Vitae (CV) ; and • A Cover Letter (maximum three A4 pages) outlining their suitability for the role. • Executive Summary (Maximum Two Pages) The Executive Summary should provide a concise overview of the applicant’s relevant experience, expertise and achievements in relation to the requirements of the position. Applicants should clearly demonstrate their suitability for the role and address the following, at a high level: o Leadership and management experience. o Capital programme, construction procurement and commercial management experience. o Public procurement, governance and regulatory compliance expertise. o Experience establishing, developing or improving procurement functions, frameworks, policies and processes. o Experience operating at a senior strategic level and influencing senior stakeholders, executive leadership teams, boards or governing authorities. o Procurement transformation, organisational improvement, digital procurement, analytics or other relevant strategic initiatives. o The scale, complexity and value of programmes, projects, procurements or portfolios managed. o The Executive Summary should highlight the applicant's most relevant experience and direct the Selection Board to supporting evidence contained within the CV and Cover Letter. Applicants who do not address these requirements will not be considered at the shortlisting stage. Any applications which are still in progress at the closing time on the specified closing date will be cancelled automatically by the system. Please see the Job Description below for this position. Informal enquiries about this post should be made to Roslyn Clohisey in the Capital Program Office at clohiser@tcd.ie Application queries about this post should be made to Recruitment Partner, Olivia McTernan at Recruit@tcd.ie and include the Competition ID number in the subject heading. Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. We welcome applications from all individuals, including those with disabilities, those who have followed non-traditional career paths, and those who have taken time out for reasons such as family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants, including those who have been displaced due to conflict or war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier university. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and hold an Athena Swan Silver Award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and across the higher education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work–life balance and creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/trrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=039546": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Philosophy",
      "description": "Closing Date: 25/11/2026 Job Title : Assistant Professor in Philosophy Pay Scale : Lecturer Merged Closing Date : 25-Nov-2026 12:00 Contact Email : recruit@tcd.ie About Trinity: Trinity is Ireland’s leading university and is ranked 75th in the world (QS World University Rankings 2026). Founded in 1592, the University is steeped in history with a reputation for excellence in education, research, and innovation, which has been inspiring generations of thinkers for over 400 years. Trinity College Dublin- an introduction Post Summary The Department of Philosophy, within the School of Social Sciences and Philosophy, invites applications for the position of Assistant Professor in Philosophy, to commence on 3 August 2027. This a tenure-track appointment of five year’s duration, with a view to permanency. The successful candidate will deliver high-quality teaching at both undergraduate and postgraduate levels, supervise and mentor students, and contribute to an engaging, inclusive and intellectually stimulating learning environment. They will be expected to teach across the Philosophy curriculum, including modules delivered to students in the Faculty of Science. The post holder will engage in high-quality research. They will also be expected to contribute to interdisciplinary curriculum development, collaborate across the School, and participate actively in the wider academic community through workshops, conferences, and other research-led initiatives. In addition, the appointee will contribute to the academic administration of the Department and School, including committee work, quality assurance processes, and other service activities that support effective academic governance. We seek applicants with vision, enthusiasm, and a commitment to excellence in both research and teaching. Candidates should demonstrate an established record of high-quality scholarship in Philosophy, along with strong potential for continued impactful research and innovation in curriculum and module design. Post Status: 5 Year Fixed Term, Tenure Track Department/Faculty: School of Social Sciences and Philosophy, Department of Philosophy Location: Trinity College Dublin, The University of Dublin, College Green, Dublin 2, Ireland Reports to: Head of Discipline and Head of School Salary: Appointment will be made on Assistant Professor Salary Scale (109) commensurate with qualifications / experience and in line with Public Sector Pay Policy [€43,859 - € 104,618 per annum] https://www.tcd.ie/hr/assets/pdf/monthly-academic.pdf Hours of Work : Hours of work for academic staff are those as prescribed under Public Service Agreements. For further information please follow the link below: http://www.tcd.ie/hr/assets/pdf/academic-hours-public-service-agreement.pdf Closing Date: 12 Noon (Irish Standard Time), 25 November 2026 Application Information Candidates must submit the application by e-recruitment: 1. Cover letter – 2 x A4 pages maximum 2. Full curriculum vitae to include the names and contact details of 3 referees 3. Research statement (summarising current research and future research plans) – 2 x A4 pages maximum 4. Teaching statement (summarising teaching experience and approach – 2 x A4 pages maximum) Please Note: • Candidates who do not submit the requested information will not be considered for shortlisting. • Any applications which are still in progress at the time of closing will automatically be cancelled by the system. • Candidates should note that the interview process for this appointment will include the delivery of a presentation as well as interview. Application will only be accepted by applying online through the Trinity Jobs Portal. Informal enquiries about this post should be made to the Head of Philosophy, Professor John Divers, via email: diversj@tcd.ie Application queries about this post, please email the Recruitment Partner at: recruit@tcd.ie and include the Competition ID number in the subject heading. Our Values - putting our words into action Values matter. They are key to the success of our Strategic Plan. We centre our behavioural values around the importance of belonging. We recognise that the university will thrive by each of us respecting and caring for one another. We know we can achieve much more through working together. We know too that we must take responsibility for our actions and be accountable. We must, and will never stop, imagining. And through being nature positive we connect not just with the environment but with our ambitions for a sustainable future. At Trinity, we are committed to equality, diversity, and inclusion. We welcome applications from all individuals, including those with disabilities, those who have followed non-traditional career paths, and those who have taken time out for reasons such as family or caring responsibilities. We also welcome international applicants, including those who have been displaced due to conflict or war. Trinity College Dublin is Ireland’s premier university. We are an EU Sustainable Gender Equality Champion and hold an Athena Swan Silver Award, recognising our ongoing work to advance gender equality both within Trinity and across the higher education sector. Trinity is committed to supporting work–life balance and creating a family-friendly working environment."
    },
